<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Empty on purpose, and it should stay that way.

	  Apple's instruction is to use only the entitlements that are absolutely
	  necessary, and spawning a *signed* child process needs none: if every
	  Mach-O in the bundle carries the same signature, library validation is
	  satisfied and posix_spawn works. The myth that a helper needs an
	  entitlement usually traces back to someone shipping an unsigned one.

	  Two that may turn out to be needed, in this order, and only if something
	  actually fails on the Mac:

	    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
	        on the AI runtime only — Metal shader compilation can allocate
	        executable memory. Add it to the helper's own plist, never to the
	        app's.

	    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
	        on `node` only, and only if better_sqlite3.node ends up signed by
	        a different identity. Signing it ourselves should make this
	        unnecessary, and Apple's own guidance says not to disable library
	        validation for executables that do not host plug-ins.

	  One that must never appear: com.apple.security.get-task-allow. Its
	  presence fails notarization outright.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
